annot(#noCore)

#noCore
# A class of functions.
  A function's name alone is the function before anything is given to it, so `every(one, isa=
  [/F])` makes that value a member of [F]. `(_ -> F)(a -> In) -> YN` says what every member gives
  once it is given an `a`, as `(_ -> S) -> T..` says what every member converts to. So a value known
  only as an [F] can be applied: [app] gives [f] its `a`, and the call is the member's own.
  [checkOne] and [checkTwo] apply each member through [app], and [checkPick] one only known to be an
  [F] until it is computed. Written without the core library, so that when a compiler change breaks
  it there are a few lines to read rather than a standard library. `classFunctionRun.ct` runs the
  same shapes.
data(Yes)
data(No)

class(YN, [Yes, No])

data(In)

class(F)

(_ -> F)(a -> In) -> YN

every(one, isa= [/F])

one(a -> In) -> Yes
one(a)= Yes

every(two, isa= [/F])

two(a -> In) -> No
two(a)= No

app(f -> F, a -> In) -> YN
app(f, a)= f(a= a)

checkOne= app(f= one, a= In)

checkTwo= app(f= two, a= In)

pick(b -> YN) -> F
pick(b= Yes)= one
pick(b= No)= two

checkPick(b -> YN) -> YN
checkPick(b)= app(f= pick(b= b), a= In)
